00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.940
- [Michael] Let's start
in the 18th century.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.560
When the drafters of the constitution

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.420
called for the creation
of the Supreme Court

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.830
as a means of balancing out

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.410
the power of the president and Congress,

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.700
the document establishes

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.260
three important things about the court.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.110
First, it gives it ultimate judicial power

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.160
while allowing Congress

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.770
to establish other lower
courts as it saw fit.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.720
Side note, it doesn't actually

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.900
explicitly say what judicial power means.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.280
Basically, leaving Congress

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.010
to eat, pray, and love
on that minor issue.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.620
Secondly, it states that
judges on these courts

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.080
shall hold their office
during good behavior.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.220
This has been interpreted to mean

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.460
that Supreme Court justices
are appointed for life

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.260
and don't have to leave unless they croak,

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.370
retire of Florida,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.100
or do something shifty
like treason or bribery.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.110
Lastly, it says that judges pay

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.510
can't be decreased while
they're on the bench

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.210
to ensure they aren't
being swayed by politicians

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.170
who could retaliate

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.960
against court decisions
by docking their pay.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.890
And that's essentially

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.600
what the founders had to say about it.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.640
It was up to Congress
to use this blueprint

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.000
and figure out what the heck
a Supreme Court was anyway.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.350
Now, believe it or not,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.440
the court was intended
to be an apolitical body,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.290
interpreting the law through
the frame of the constitution

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.990
and not any partisan set of beliefs.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.280
Seriously, that's not a bit.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.710
That's really what it was supposed to be.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.620
The constitution was also really
vague on details regarding

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.350
the establishment of federal
courts outside of SCOTUS.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.300
This may have been on purpose.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.580
As law professors, Richard W. Garnett

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.907
and David A. Strauss put it,

00:03:39.907 --> 00:03:42.390
"To some people in the
United States at that time,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:43.380
the federal government

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.060
seemed almost like a foreign government.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.090
Those people's main loyalty
was to their states."

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.160
As a result, a network of federal courts

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.830
with elite judges
handpicked by the president,

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.577
legislating in far-flung states,

00:03:54.577 --> 00:03:57.360
"Amounted to allowing the
foreign federal government

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.660
to get its tentacles into
every corner of the nation."

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.970
So, the issue was delicate.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.180
On the one hand, who can
blame people for being worried

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.010
that a bunch of hoity-toity elites

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.360
with no ties to their communities

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:10.830
can make decisions for them.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.080
On the other hand, some of the framers

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.260
were concerned that leaving
too much power to state courts

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.670
meant that hostile states

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.860
could undermine the laws of the land.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.260
Thus, Congress determined
that state courts

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:23.910
could get a fairly wide birth

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.220
in figuring out local laws for themselves.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.010
At the same time, they
acknowledged that the Supreme Court

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.980
established the supreme laws of the land

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.480
and would have the power to intervene

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.720
if a state court made an
unconstitutional ruling.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.820
Now, none of this would be the final say

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.130
on what SCOTUS became because,

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.020
and I cannot stress this enough,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.730
very little about the Supreme Court

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.860
is in our actual constitution.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.250
And early Congress had
a lot to figure out,

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.840
like the makeup of the court,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.880
including the number
of justices and so on.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.880
Consequently, the court's
responsibilities and jurisdictions

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.670
have changed multiple
times since its inception.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.260
So, what has been the function

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.300
of the Supreme Court in America

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.610
and how has that function changed.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.360
And has the institution over
time become too powerful?

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.380
To figure that out,

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.810
let's look at one of the most
pivotal moments for the court,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.240
which occurred in its
tentative early days.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.550
A paradigm-changing Supreme
Court decision was reached

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.470
under chief justice, John Marshall,

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.303
who had been appointed

00:05:20.303 --> 00:05:22.620
during some characteristically
petty theatrics

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.810
between outgoing president, John Adams,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.240
and his nemesis, Thomas Jefferson.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.130
After Jefferson defeated Adams,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.750
the lame duck, present as Congress,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.030
passed the Judiciary Act of 1801,

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.550
which expanded the number of
federal courts and judges.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.190
Adams immediately used this new law

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.680
to appoint a bunch of midnight judges,

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.690
one surprisingly favored him.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.060
But there wasn't enough
time to formally carry out

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.160
all these appointments
before Adam left office.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.350
Those appointees not
officially commissioned,

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:52.470
were in Jefferson's eyes,
rendered null and void.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.000
One snob appointee

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.880
was would-be DC Justice of
the Peace William Marbury.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.860
He took the issue to
Marshall Supreme Court,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.460
which found that indeed,
Marbury had a right to the job.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.440
But Marshall wasn't sure if it was really

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.050
the Supreme Court's place
to make this ruling.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.450
To TLDR This legalese,

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.550
the court decided that for the most part,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.530
it did not have original jurisdiction,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.220
meaning it couldn't rule on
a case for the first time,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.606
but it did have appellate jurisdiction.

00:06:19.606 --> 00:06:23.250
That is the authority to
rule on a case on appeal.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.310
So, while Marbury was correct
about his right to the job,

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.030
SCOTUS did not have
jurisdiction to make that call,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.880
at least not until it
reached the appeal stage.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.620
Now, it might sound like the Supreme Court

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.390
had ruled to limit its power,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.100
but what it really did was establish

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.640
the incredibly important
concept of judicial review,

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.740
the power of the court
to look at decisions

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.090
made by the other branches

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.740
of the government and other courts

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.380
and determine whether or
not they are constitutional.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.900
Their ruling made it official
that the constitution

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.520
wasn't just a set of guidelines,

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.460
but was actually the
ultimate law of the land.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.130
And it was up to the Supreme
Court to decide what it meant

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.410
and if daddy government
was using it right.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.520
This seems to be in line

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.890
with what several of the framers believed,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.590
including Alexander
Hamilton and James Madison.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.440
In theory, this all means
that the court is nonpartisan

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.940
and exists solely to make sure

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.430
everyone's constitutional
rights are granted.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.520
That sounds great and non-controversial.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.260
But when you tie your decision-making

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.760
to an unchanging document,

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.420
you set yourself up to be the
voice of a certain status quo,

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:25.253
which is pretty much

00:07:25.253 --> 00:07:27.360
what SCOTUS has been
since it's early days.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.780
What's more, as scholars,
Lawrence Baum and Neal Devins,

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.820
arguing their study of outside influence

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.140
over the court's decisions,

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.570
the Supreme Court is disincentivized

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:39.480
from making bold rulings
that might be controversial,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.630
specifically because it
doesn't have any ability

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.760
to actually carry out its own decisions.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.250
There's no Supreme Court police force,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.802
except for the buff ghost
of Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:50.903
- So, today, we're gonna be working

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:52.380
all the major muscle groups to the bottom,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.380
that the abs, gams, tuchus,

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.550
chicken wings
(chicken clucks)

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.140
and critical thinking.
(bell dings)

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.390
- And there's no team of
supervising bureaucrats

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:02.880
to ensure that these rulings
are actually enacted.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.260
Thus, the fear of backlash

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.070
against an unpopular opinion
has always loomed large.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.223
As the researchers explain,

00:08:09.223 --> 00:08:12.510
this can often lead to justices
making narrower rulings

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.430
in order to avoid too much controversy.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:15.840
The result has been that the court

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.820
is a relatively conservative body,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.550
not in a sense of
Republican versus Democrat,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.490
but in terms of change versus status quo.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:26.107
As Keeanga-Yamahtta Taylor
writes for "The New Yorker",

00:08:26.107 --> 00:08:27.360
"As the branch of government

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.700
that is least accountable
to the American public,

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.280
the Supreme Court is tended,
for most of its history,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.380
toward a fundamental conservatism,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.820
siding with tradition

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:38.220
over more expansive
visions of human rights."

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.570
This makes sense, because again,

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.210
they're applying an
increasingly old document

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.550
to any given contemporary scenario.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.920
This was certainly the case
when it came to slavery,

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.160
which was deemed a question
best left to the states.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.310
In 1857, when the court
ruled that enslaved people

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.110
were property, rather than citizens,

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.630
the majority of judges were slave owners.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.370
Their take wasn't an aberration.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.080
While slavery isn't explicitly

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.340
mentioned in the constitution,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.260
it did specifically protect

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.110
the importing of slaves up until 1808.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.720
And the Supreme Court
openly perpetuated the idea

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.940
of enslaved people as protective property,

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.010
even striking down a Pennsylvania law

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.730
that banned using violence
to collect escaped slaves.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.657
As Erwin Chemerinsky writes in his book,

00:09:19.657 --> 00:09:21.307
"The Case Against the Supreme Court",

00:09:21.307 --> 00:09:23.760
"At every opportunity until the Civil War,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:24.720
the Supreme Court acted

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.460
to protect the rights of slave owners

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.950
and denied all rights to
those who were enslaved."

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:32.330
That's 70 years of going
to bat for guys like this.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.610
- He is a rambunctious sword, ain't he?

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.520
- You might be thinking,
that was a long time ago.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.170
Things are way better now.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.703
And yes, the court no
longer sanctioned slavery,

00:09:42.703 --> 00:09:46.170
but it did take a whole
Civil War to get there.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.350
And obviously, ending
slavery didn't end racism.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.500
And Chemerinsky notes that an
important part of the story

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.890
is that the court did nothing
to advance racial equality

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.830
for almost 90 years after the Civil War,

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.750
and instead, used its power and influence

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:04.230
to limit the protections of
the post-Civil War amendments.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.330
A prime example of this was the separate

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.120
but equal decision
under Plessy v. Ferguson

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.710
that facilitated segregation

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.170
to devastating effects
on the black community.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.030
And the court siding with say,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.370
plantation owners over enslaved people,

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:19.470
wasn't a historical aberration.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.480
It's illustrative of a larger tendency.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.830
In cases where business

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.350
or corporate interests clash
with those of the common man,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.740
the court has historically
cited with those empowered.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.530
This was illustrated acutely
during the Gilded Age.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.990
From about 1890 to the 1930s,

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.780
the court embraced the
laissez-faire social Darwinism

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.000
that dominated political
thought at the time.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.400
They declared freedom of contract,

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.590
the freedom to independently negotiate

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:46.200
your own business contract,
a constitutional right,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.840
which allowed them to
strike down tons of laws

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:50.099
that would've protected workers

00:10:50.099 --> 00:10:51.030
(brick falling)
and consumers

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.200
from big corporations.
(cartoon screaming)

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.180
The thinking was, if you were an employer

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:56.160
and your state passed a minimum wage law,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.300
that was an infringement
(scissor snipping)

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:00.540
on your right to set your
own employment terms.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:01.680
They struck down over

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.090
200 federal state
(punching sound effect)

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:04.860
and local economic regulations

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:08.550
to protect the sacred right
of powerful corporations.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.980
The court also took huge
steps to limit federal power

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.540
to protect the most vulnerable.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:13.980
For example, they decided

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:15.090
that a federal ban
(horn blaring)

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.740
on interstate shipment of goods

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.920
produced by child labor
was unconstitutional

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.680
because that should be
left up to the states.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.260
At the time, states that
wanted to ban child labor

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.750
risked losing their
competitive business advantages

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.700
to states that engaged in it.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.907
Economic pressure, those kept
child labor alive and well.

00:11:32.907 --> 00:11:34.980
And the country saw millions of children

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.380
working in deadly conditions for decades.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.230
Now, they just have to go to
school for deadly conditions.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.940
We're not gonna put that in the video,

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.100
But it's (beep) and it's
dark, and it's true.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:45.540
So, you know what? I dare you, editor.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.340
Put it in there. Speak truth to power.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:49.350
Note that just a decade earlier,

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.180
the court had ruled
that interstate shipment

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.290
of lottery tickets

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.123
could be banned
(error sound effect)

00:11:53.123 --> 00:11:54.000
by the federal government,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.230
but hey, protecting the nation

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:56.580
from out-of-state lottery tickets,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.370
but not from child labor
seems totally normal

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:00.420
and reasonable.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:02.610
Again, what we're seeing
in this time period

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.340
is a general reluctance to
protect vulnerable people

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.080
from government or corporate power.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:08.610
It's easy now to look back

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.680
and lament the way the
Supreme court functioned

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.780
pre-Civil war or in the Gilded Age,

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:13.852
but it's worth more closely examining

00:12:13.852 --> 00:12:15.930
what happened under the court

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.330
in its more progressive errors of history.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:20.730
Take Chief Justice Earl Warren's court,

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.350
often considered the last one

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.260
to truly represent the
interest of the common man.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:26.700
Earlier courts weren't known

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.530
for expanding civil rights and liberties,

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.770
but Warren was openly
committed to social justice.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.080
Once more, he was likable and persuasive,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.810
which led to even some of
the most controversial cases

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.370
that came across SCOTUS' desk,

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.460
reaching unanimous or
near unanimous decisions

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:42.510
from the justices,

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:46.110
which is basically unheard of
today for such divisive cases.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.640
Brown v. Board of Education,

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:49.860
the landmark decision, which
reversed to the separate,

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.680
but equal doctrine, was
decided unanimously.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:54.930
This is directly because
of Warren's influence.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.480
According to another
Justice's autobiography,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.880
had the Supreme Court ruled in 1952

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.320
when the case was initially argued

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.510
and before Warren joined the court,

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:07.500
there would've been a 5-4
vote to uphold segregation.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.360
What in the actual (beep)
What in the actual (beep)

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.190
Then there was Gideon v. Wainwright,

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:15.810
which found that everyone has the right

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.520
to legal counsel in a criminal case.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:18.990
And Loving v. Virginia,

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:19.920
which ruled that laws

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.470
banning interracial marriage
were unconstitutional.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:25.290
These decisions helped create
a constitutional precedent

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:26.280
that allowed Congress

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.860
to pass other important
pieces of legislation

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.200
like the Civil Rights Act of 1964

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.720
and the Voting Rights Act of 1965.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:36.420
But not everyone was
happy with this progress,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.870
especially the
definitely-not-a-crook Richard Nixon.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:43.260
Nixon maintained a 30-year
beef with Earl Warren,

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:44.640
who had refused to endorse him

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.260
earlier in his political career.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.060
So, when Nixon became president,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.360
he wanted to tear down
everything that Warren had built.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.370
And due to some squabbling
that foreshadowed

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:56.430
Barack Obama's nomination of
Merrick Garland being squashed,

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:58.530
Nixon was able to fill four seats

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.420
over the course of his short presidency.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:04.260
His goal he said, was to a
point strict constructionists.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:05.287
These guys would be,

00:14:05.287 --> 00:14:08.100
"Interpreting the law, not making the law.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:11.220
They would see themselves as
caretakers of the constitution

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:12.390
and servants of the people,

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:14.790
not super-legislators with a free hand

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.340
to impose their political
and social viewpoints."

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:20.730
At first glance, this sounds
relatively apolitical,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:22.320
but it was actually the first glimmer

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:23.760
of an important philosophy

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.430
that would change the way
the Supreme Court functioned.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:29.250
And Nixon's strict constructionist judges

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.690
were expected to roll back

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.810
some of the Warren Court's
broad constructionist decisions

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:35.160
to expand human rights.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.710
That's why things like
abortion or same sex marriage

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.990
aren't ruled expressly
legal by the Supreme Court,

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:42.810
but instead, use constitutional reasoning

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.960
like the equal protection
clause and the right to privacy.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.850
This is also what makes any rights

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.150
not expressly delineated in
the constitution so tenuous,

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:53.310
even if they're popular with citizens.

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:55.770
And while Nixon's appointee,
Chief justice Burger,

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:57.870
did not roll back the
Warren Court's decisions

00:14:57.870 --> 00:14:59.280
on mass as expected,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.550
his appointment cemented an
ideological shift in the court.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.590
It went from expanding civil liberties

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.300
to seeing such expansions of rights

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.150
as outside of the purview
of the constitution.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.560
Like it's earlier iterations,

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.350
this new court regularly
ruled in favor of corporate

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:16.320
and institutional interest
against regular citizens.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.660
Today, the Supreme Court
has several justices

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.520
who follow strict constructionism,

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.500
specifically what is called textualism,

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:23.940
which holds that the constitution

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.550
should be interpreted the
way your average person

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:29.190
in 1787 would've interpreted it.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:32.160
How does that account for say,
the rise of global capitalism

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.490
or the invention of the AK-47
or the rise of the internet

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:36.630
you might be wondering?

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:37.590
It doesn't really.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:39.390
But surely, you must be wondering

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.330
what to make of those few
exciting Supreme Court cases

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:43.860
you learned about in high school,

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.230
which takes us back to the Warren Court,

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.662
because it evokes a more romantic
vision of the institution.

00:15:49.662 --> 00:15:52.890
And yet even some of its
most groundbreaking decisions

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.620
look mealy-mouthed in retrospect

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:56.940
Take Brown v. Board,

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.670
which ended legal government
mandated segregation.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:03.450
Surely, we're not going to
critique this ruling, right?

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.160
Well, in reality,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:08.460
while the Warren Court struck
down lots of individual cases

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.710
brought to them about
segregation in courtrooms,

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:13.500
on buses, on golf courses,
and restaurants, and so on,

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:16.470
they never wrote an opinion
that said state governments

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:18.750
actually had to do anything

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.570
to actively end segregation
or prevent it from happening.

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:22.770
Quite the opposite, in fact.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.180
As Robert L. Carter

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.407
wrote in the "Michigan
Law Review" in 1968,

00:16:26.407 --> 00:16:28.200
"The court ruled that a state

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.390
is under no affirmative obligation

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:32.700
to enact anti-discrimination legislation

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.950
and that such laws may be repealed;

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:37.650
but the court added the
precautionary caveat

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.510
that if in the process of repeal

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:41.010
the state tips the political balance

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:42.990
in favor of racial discrimination,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.910
it violates the 14th Amendment."

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.970
So, a state not only
didn't have to put any laws

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.380
in place to end discrimination,

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.060
but they also were within their rights

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:53.670
to repeal anti-discrimination legislation,

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.440
as long as they didn't do an oopsie

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.890
and tip the scales towards
intentional discrimination,

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:01.050
which is totally a very easy

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:03.390
and objective thing to
measure and prevent.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.490
Thus, I'm sure you'll be shocked to hear

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.920
that virtually no school desegregation

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.380
actually happened for years after Brown.

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:12.960
It wasn't until the
Civil Rights Act of 1964

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:16.110
that any meaningful progress
was made in desegregation,

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:18.450
and that was limited mostly
to cities in the south.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.660
In San Antonio Independent
School District v. Rodriguez,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:24.030
SCOTUS even went so far as to rule

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:26.820
that inequalities in school
funding aren't illegal

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:28.110
because get this,

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:32.100
the right to equal education
isn't in the constitution.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.000
That's right.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:35.400
SCOTUS ruled that it's fine for schools

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:38.010
to be both separate and unequal

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:41.550
as long as the segregation isn't
mandated by the government.

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:43.350
And in the 50 years since Brown,

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.480
the Supreme Court has
made America's segregation

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.600
and inequality problems even worse.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.090
Like the 1991 decision

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:51.990
in which the Oklahoma City School Board

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.210
ceased busing black
students to white schools

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.530
as a desegregation effort

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.780
because schools had
become less segregated.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:58.830
And surprisingly,
(error sound effect)

00:17:58.830 --> 00:17:59.940
when the busing ended,

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.520
the schools became segregated again.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.350
And while there's plenty
wrong with busing,

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.670
SCOTUS-ruled schools

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.920
don't have to be equal
in terms of funding,

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.270
and you can probably guess
(coins clinks)

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.070
whose schools weren't being funded.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:14.520
Still, the court ruled that
federal acts like busing

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:16.620
were only meant as a temporary measure

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.330
to remedy past discrimination.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:19.650
So, because black students

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.810
have been given their rights
for the past 13 years,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:23.250
the Supreme Court assumed

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:26.430
that the racial problem at
the root had been fixed.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.530
What this really meant was that schools

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.810
stopped being segregated by law,

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.990
but remained effectively
segregated by where people lived.

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.300
And because it was fine
for schools to be unequal,

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:37.140
black and Latino
(cartoon screaming)

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:38.520
students suffered.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.440
Overall, if you look at the Supreme Court

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:41.820
over the past 50 years,

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:44.910
you can see how a general
passivity towards granting rights

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.470
has implicitly stripped

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.960
many of those rights
away from many Americans.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:50.130
Take the time in 2011

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:53.100
when a class action lawsuit
against Walmart was filed

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:56.490
on behalf of 1.5 million women,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.230
arguing that they were paid less than

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.660
and had to wait longer
for promotions than men.

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:03.420
Institutionally, there
was no application process

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.700
for management and
employees were handpicked.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.460
Unsurprisingly, this resulted
in pay raises and promotions

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:10.440
being disproportionately given to men.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:12.660
And while on three occasions,

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:14.850
the US Court of Appeals
for the Ninth Circuit

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:18.180
upheld that these women should
be certified as a class,

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:20.010
SCOTUS ruled that there were just

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:20.940
too many of them
(error sound effect)

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:24.030
to have nothing common to
justify a class action suit.

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:25.620
If they wanted to come after Walmart,

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.840
they should do it individually instead.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.440
As Adam Cohen points out in
his book, "Supreme Injustice",

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:33.390
the whole point of a class action lawsuit

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:36.270
is that individuals can
collectively share claims

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.310
and show ongoing patterns of wrongdoing

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.680
without forking out their
life savings for a lawyer.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.740
It is nearly impossible
for one person to show

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.560
that they have individually
been discriminated against.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.280
Whereas a large representative
group demonstrates a pattern.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:52.530
What appears to be a neutral decision

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:56.010
ends up being a huge loss
for average Walmart workers

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:58.710
and a huge win for a massive corporation.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:00.930
According to studies,
the current Roberts Court

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.930
has been the most business-friendly
in nearly a century,

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.730
and it's not due to party politics.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.070
Liberal and conservative judges alike

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:10.530
seem to be able to agree
on corporate rights.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:13.110
Meanwhile, decisions that
pit everyday Americans

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.690
against big corporations
and powerful institutions

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:17.310
like the police and prisons

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:18.690
leave us with little recourse

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:20.310
to seek justice for individuals

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.410
because in the eyes of the Supreme Court,

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.690
the constitution is fair.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.570
Take the decision in
Citizens United v. FEC,

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:29.610
which said that corporations
had the same rights

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.430
as individuals to donate
money to political candidates.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:33.810
In the decades that followed,

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.930
wealthy donors and Super PACs
of every political persuasion

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:39.930
have spent over $3
billion to sway elections

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:42.270
toward candidates that will
protect their interests.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.700
While hypothetically,
everyone has the right

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.410
to donate to their candidates of choice,

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:48.270
we sadly don't have the right

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:50.850
to the type of money
that PACs have access to.

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:54.270
Thus, the decision inherently
favors one group of people,

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:55.470
the mega wealthy.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.180
This decision isn't too surprising

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:00.330
when you consider that 70%
of Supreme Court Justices

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:03.360
in the past 50 years have
been Ivy League graduates.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.610
As sociologist G. William Domhoff

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.537
writes in his book, "Who Rules America?",

00:21:07.537 --> 00:21:08.880
"There is both a funneling

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:11.490
and homogenizing effect
from these schools."

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:12.457
This effect, he says,

00:21:12.457 --> 00:21:14.550
"Plays out in terms of social networks,

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:15.840
cultural/social capital,

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:18.300
and a feeling of being
part of the in-group."

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.520
Scholars Baum and Devins
take this further,

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.040
arguing that Supreme Court
Justice's demographics

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:25.470
have material effects on how they rule.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.540
They write, "Supreme Court justices

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:29.250
care more about elite audiences

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:30.870
than they do about the mass public."

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:32.280
As they note, "Like others,

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:35.340
Supreme Court justices want
most to be liked and respected

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.377
by people to whom they
are personally close

00:21:37.377 --> 00:21:39.150
and people whom they identify.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:40.020
For the justices,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:43.050
those people are overwhelmingly
part of elite groups."

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:45.360
And then, as system is set
up for checks and balances

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.310
between the three branches of government,

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:50.760
many argue that SCOTUS wields
disproportionate power,

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:53.010
including power over
the will of the people

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.010
since they're able to make
wildly unpopular decisions

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.080
that don't reflect the majority opinions

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:59.220
of the American public.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:03.510
There's over 300 million
of us and nine of them.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:04.710
According to Mickey Edwards,

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:06.360
a former eight-term member of Congress

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.610
and chairman of the House
Republican Policy Committee,

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:10.560
the Supreme Court, "Is theoretically

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.470
and constitutionally
separate and independent.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.290
But in reality, the
separateness is a bit fuzzy.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.260
That's because even in this
age of hyperpartisanship,

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:21.420
there is at least one important area

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.090
in which Democrats and
Republicans think alike:

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.860
both parties view the federal courts,

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:27.360
and especially the Supreme Court

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.700
as a defacto branch of the legislature."

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:32.400
In other words, while
everyone may talk a big game

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:35.100
about the political neutrality
of the Supreme Court,

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:36.630
politicians of every stripe

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:38.730
have come to understand that in reality,

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.400
whichever party appoints the most justices

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.540
wields the most power,

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.160
particularly in a Congress

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.440
too divided to do much of anything.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.750
Does this sound undemocratic?

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:51.480
Well, that may have
been the original point

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.090
At the New York Ratifying
Convention for the constitution,

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:55.987
Hamilton himself said that,

00:22:55.987 --> 00:22:58.230
"There are few positions,
more demonstrable

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:00.480
than that there should
be, in every Republic,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.660
some permanent body to
correct the prejudices,

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.130
check the intemperate passions,

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:08.220
and regulate the fluctuations
of a popular assembly."

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.650
This is basically fancy colonial talk

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.330
for a rather chilling argument,

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:14.430
that we need special appointed people

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:17.340
to protect us from our literal democracy.

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.220
On side note, classic
Greek philosopher, Plato,

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:21.660
would largely agree on this.
(bell dings)

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.077
Or as Chemerinsky puts it,

00:23:23.077 --> 00:23:25.490
"The primary reason for
having a Supreme Court then

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.210
is to enforce the constitution

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:29.430
against the will of the majority."

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:31.920
This seems true in the
expected decision ruling

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:33.030
against Roe V. Wade,

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.940
which 64% of the country
wants to see upheld.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.410
You may be thinking, well,

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:38.893
then we should change the constitution.

00:23:38.893 --> 00:23:40.964
But this was by design,

00:23:40.964 --> 00:23:43.920
made very, very, very difficult to do.

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:44.910
So, in a lot of ways,

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:47.130
we're all still operating by a playbook

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:50.190
written by slave owners in the late 1700s.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:51.577
As Chemerinsky adds,

00:23:51.577 --> 00:23:54.750
"Why should a society generally
committed to majority rule

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:56.910
choose to be governed by a document

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:59.070
that is very difficult to change?"

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:01.470
And we've only grazed
the tip of the iceberg.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.810
If you dig deeper, you'll
find a history of the court

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.960
supporting things like eugenics,
Japanese internment camps,

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:10.320
severely limiting free speech
in times of war, and so on.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:12.990
So, is the Supreme Court beyond fixing?

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.880
There are senators and representatives

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:18.270
who have proposed changes
such as expanding the court,

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:19.560
imposing term limits,

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.840
and other measures to
curb the massive power

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.420
currently afforded to every
Supreme Court Justice.

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.340
Of course, with the
filibuster still in place,

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:27.480
putting any of these changes

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.220
in motion is basically impossible.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:30.150
And all of these measures

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.700
seem like insurmountably uphill battles.

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.730
But if the court continues
to make unpopular decisions,

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.280
maybe it'll finally spur
the kind of backlash

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.830
they've been dreading since 1787.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.440
- So, you're telling me there's a chance?

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:45.030
- But what do you guys think?

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:48.630
Are the nine perfect strangers
not so perfect after all?

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.060
Or should we give them
more credit for legislating

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:53.520
with less than ideal guidance
from the founding fathers?

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.290
Let us know in the comments.

